\subsection{Development Decisions and Rationale}
\label{subsec:development-decisions}

This section documents the key development decisions made during implementation of the Solar Energy Monitoring and Control System.

\subsubsection{Technology Stack Selection}
\label{subsubsec:tech-decisions}

\paragraph{Backend Technology}
The system utilizes Java 21 with Spring Boot 3.3 as the primary backend technology. This choice was driven by the need for:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Strong type safety and compile-time error detection
    \item Mature ecosystem with comprehensive security framework integration
    \item Excellent performance characteristics for real-time energy monitoring
    \item Native support for reactive programming patterns required for IoT data streams
\end{itemize}

Spring Data JPA was implemented for database operations, providing automatic repository implementation and reducing boilerplate code while supporting complex energy data queries.

\paragraph{Frontend Technology}
Next.js 14 with React 18 and TypeScript was selected for frontend development to provide:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Server-side rendering for improved initial load performance
    \item Built-in routing and API routes for simplified architecture
    \item TypeScript compile-time type checking for energy data handling
    \item Excellent developer experience with hot reloading
\end{itemize}

Tailwind CSS was chosen for styling to enable utility-first approach and consistent responsive design across different device types commonly used in solar installations.

\subsubsection{Implementation Patterns}
\label{subsubsec:implementation-patterns}

\paragraph{Modular Architecture}
The system implements a modular architecture with five core modules:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{User Management}: Authentication and authorization
    \item \textbf{Energy Monitoring}: Real-time data collection and processing
    \item \textbf{Payment Compliance}: Automated billing and payment processing
    \item \textbf{Tampering Detection}: Security monitoring and anomaly detection
    \item \textbf{Service Control}: Remote device management and control
\end{itemize}

\paragraph{Data Processing Strategy}
Energy data processing follows a multi-layered approach:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Real-time data ingestion through REST endpoints
    \item Asynchronous processing for high-volume energy readings
    \item Automated aggregation for daily, weekly, and monthly summaries
    \item Efficient querying through indexed database structures
\end{itemize}


\subsection{Database Design}
\label{subsec:database-design}

The Solar Energy Monitoring and Control System employs a comprehensive relational database design optimized for solar energy monitoring, payment compliance, and system security. The database schema is organized around core business entities with well-defined relationships to ensure data integrity and efficient querying.

\subsubsection{Entity-Relationship Model}
