|   +-- Verifies: Offline device detection algorithms
|
+-- calculateSystemHealth_MultipleMetrics_ReturnsHealthScore()
    +-- Input: Various system performance metrics
    +-- Expected: Calculated system health score
    +-- Verifies: Health scoring algorithms
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{Performance Testing Analysis}
\label{subsec:performance-testing}

Although comprehensive performance testing has not been implemented in the current version, this section provides an analysis of expected performance characteristics and scalability considerations based on the system design.

\subsubsection{Load Testing Scenarios}
\label{subsubsec:load-scenarios}

\paragraph{Energy Data Ingestion Performance}

\textbf{Expected Performance Characteristics:}
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Target Throughput}: 1,000 energy readings per second
    \item \textbf{Response Time}: < 200ms for 95\% of requests
    \item \textbf{Concurrent Devices}: Support for 10,000 simultaneous device connections
    \item \textbf{Data Volume}: Processing of 86.4 million readings per day (1,000 devices × 86,400 seconds)
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Performance Bottlenecks Analysis:}
\begin{verbatim}
Potential Bottlenecks:
+-- Database Write Operations
|   +-- Issue: High volume of INSERT operations
|   +-- Mitigation: Batch inserts, connection pooling
|   +-- Optimization: Partitioned tables, optimized indexes
|
+-- JSON Parsing and Validation
|   +-- Issue: CPU-intensive request processing
|   +-- Mitigation: Efficient JSON libraries, schema validation
|   +-- Optimization: Request caching, validation optimization
|
+-- Network I/O
    +-- Issue: High network traffic from device communications
    +-- Mitigation: Connection pooling, keep-alive headers
    +-- Optimization: Compression, optimized payload sizes
\end{verbatim}

\paragraph{Dashboard Query Performance}

\textbf{Expected Performance Characteristics:}
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Query Response Time}: < 500ms for dashboard data
    \item \textbf{Concurrent Users}: Support for 1,000 simultaneous dashboard users
    \item \textbf{Data Aggregation}: Real-time aggregation of up to 30 days of data
    \item \textbf{Chart Rendering}: < 2 seconds for complex energy charts
\end{itemize}

\paragraph{Payment Processing Performance}

\textbf{Expected Performance Characteristics:}
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Transaction Processing}: < 3 seconds per payment transaction
    \item \textbf{Concurrent Payments}: Support for 100 simultaneous payment processes
    \item \textbf{External API Calls}: < 5 seconds for external payment provider communication
    \item \textbf{Database Transactions}: < 1 second for payment record creation
\end{itemize}

\subsubsection{Scalability Analysis}
\label{subsubsec:scalability-analysis}

\paragraph{Horizontal Scaling Capabilities}

\textbf{Application Tier Scaling:}
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Stateless Design}: Application servers are stateless and can be horizontally scaled
    \item \textbf{Load Balancing}: Multiple application instances can be deployed behind a load balancer
    \item \textbf{Session Management}: JWT tokens eliminate server-side session state requirements
    \item \textbf{Resource Utilization}: Each application instance requires approximately 1GB RAM and 1 CPU core
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Database Tier Scaling:}
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Read Replicas}: Support for read-only database replicas for query scaling
    \item \textbf{Partitioning}: Time-series data partitioned by date for improved query performance
    \item \textbf{Connection Pooling}: Optimized connection management to support high concurrency
    \item \textbf{Caching Strategy}: Redis or similar caching layer for frequently accessed data
\end{itemize}

\paragraph{Vertical Scaling Limits}

\textbf{Memory Requirements:}
\begin{verbatim}
Memory Scaling Analysis:
+-- Base Application: 512MB minimum, 2GB recommended
+-- Database Connections: 10MB per 100 connections
+-- Caching Layer: 1GB for frequently accessed data
+-- JVM Heap: 1.5GB for optimal garbage collection
+-- Operating System: 512MB for system processes

Total Recommended: 4GB RAM per application instance
\end{verbatim}

\textbf{CPU Requirements:}
\begin{verbatim}
CPU Scaling Analysis:
+-- Request Processing: 2 cores for baseline load
+-- Background Processing: 1 core for scheduled tasks
+-- Database Operations: Scales with concurrent users
+-- JSON Processing: CPU-intensive during peak loads

Total Recommended: 4 CPU cores per application instance
\end{verbatim}

\subsubsection{High-Volume Performance Projections}
\label{subsubsec:high-volume-projections}

\paragraph{Large-Scale Deployment Scenarios}

\textbf{Scenario 1: 100,000 Solar Installations}
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Daily Energy Readings}: 8.64 billion records per day
    \item \textbf{Database Growth}: ~50GB per month (assuming 600 bytes per reading)
    \item \textbf{Required Infrastructure}: 20 application servers, 5 database servers
    \item \textbf{Network Bandwidth}: 100 Mbps sustained, 500 Mbps peak
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Scenario 2: 1 Million Solar Installations}
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Daily Energy Readings}: 86.4 billion records per day
    \item \textbf{Database Growth}: ~500GB per month
    \item \textbf{Required Infrastructure}: 200 application servers, 50 database servers
    \item \textbf{Network Bandwidth}: 1 Gbps sustained, 5 Gbps peak
\end{itemize}

\paragraph{Performance Optimization Strategies}

\textbf{Database Optimization:}
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Partitioning Strategy}: Monthly partitions for time-series data
    \item \textbf{Index Optimization}: Composite indexes on frequently queried columns
    \item \textbf{Archive Strategy}: Archive data older than 2 years to separate storage
    \item \textbf{Query Optimization}: Materialized views for complex aggregations
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Application Optimization:}
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Caching Strategy}: Multi-level caching with Redis and in-memory caches
    \item \textbf{Async Processing}: Asynchronous processing for non-critical operations
    \item \textbf{Batch Operations}: Batch processing for bulk data operations
    \item \textbf{Connection Pooling}: Optimized database connection management
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Testing Lessons Learned}
\label{subsec:testing-lessons}

This section documents key insights and lessons learned during the development and testing process, which have influenced implementation decisions and future planning.

\subsubsection{Development Process Insights}
\label{subsubsec:development-insights}

\paragraph{Test-Driven Development Benefits}

\textbf{Positive Outcomes:}
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Early Bug Detection}: Unit tests caught integration issues before system testing
    \item \textbf{Code Quality}: TDD approach led to better-designed, more modular code
    \item \textbf{Refactoring Confidence}: Comprehensive test coverage enabled safe code refactoring
    \item \textbf{Documentation Value}: Tests serve as executable documentation of expected behavior
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Challenges Encountered:}
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Initial Overhead}: Writing tests initially slowed development but paid dividends later
    \item \textbf{Mock Complexity}: Complex mocking required for services with many dependencies
    \item \textbf{Test Maintenance}: Tests required updates when business requirements changed
\end{itemize}

\paragraph{Integration Testing Experiences}

\textbf{Key Findings:}
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Database Testing}: Using H2 in-memory database simplified test setup but missed PostgreSQL-specific issues
    \item \textbf{API Testing}: MockMvc provided excellent testing capabilities for REST endpoints
    \item \textbf{Authentication Testing}: Mock authentication simplified testing but required careful security validation
    \item \textbf{Error Handling}: Integration tests revealed edge cases not caught by unit tests
\end{itemize}

\subsubsection{Performance Testing Insights}
\label{subsubsec:performance-insights}

Although comprehensive performance testing was not implemented, development-time performance analysis revealed several insights:

\paragraph{Database Performance Observations}

\textbf{Query Performance:}
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Time-Series Queries}: Queries involving date ranges showed significant performance improvements with proper indexing
    \item \textbf{Aggregation Operations}: Complex energy summaries benefited from database-level aggregation rather than application-level processing
    \item \textbf{Join Operations}: Careful optimization of JOIN operations between installations and energy data improved response times
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Data Volume Considerations:}
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Insert Performance}: Batch inserts performed significantly better than individual INSERT operations
    \item \textbf{Index Maintenance}: Adding indexes improved query performance but slightly impacted insert performance
    \item \textbf{Connection Pooling}: Proper connection pool configuration was crucial for handling concurrent requests
\end{itemize}

\paragraph{Application Performance Observations}

\textbf{Memory Usage:}
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Object Creation}: Frequent creation of DTO objects during JSON processing impacted garbage collection
    \item \textbf{Caching Benefits}: Implementing application-level caching reduced database load significantly
    \item \textbf{Stream Processing}: Using Java Streams for data processing improved code readability but required memory considerations for large datasets
\end{itemize}

\subsubsection{Testing Strategy Evolution}
\label{subsubsec:testing-evolution}

\paragraph{Planned Testing Enhancements}

\textbf{End-to-End Testing (Future):}
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Browser Automation}: Selenium or Playwright for complete user workflow testing
    \item \textbf{API Testing}: Comprehensive API testing with tools like Newman or Postman collections
    \item \textbf{Device Simulation}: Enhanced Python simulation scripts for realistic device behavior testing
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Performance Testing (Future):}
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Load Testing}: JMeter scripts for simulating high-volume energy data ingestion
    \item \textbf{Stress Testing}: Identifying system breaking points under extreme load
    \item \textbf{Endurance Testing}: Long-running tests to identify memory leaks and performance degradation
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Security Testing (Future):}
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Penetration Testing}: Automated security testing with tools like OWASP ZAP
    \item \textbf{Authentication Testing}: Comprehensive testing of JWT token security and session management
    \item \textbf{Input Validation}: Systematic testing of all input validation and sanitization
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Current Testing Limitations}
\label{subsec:testing-limitations}

The current version of the Solar Energy Monitoring and Control System implements only unit testing and integration testing as part of its testing strategy. The testing focus has been on verifying core functionality and component interactions within the backend services.

While a more comprehensive testing approach might include end-to-end testing, performance testing, and security testing, these have not been implemented in the current version. The current implementation is focused on demonstrating core functionality rather than production readiness.

\textbf{Specific Limitations:}
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Email Service Integration}: No email server integration implemented. All email functionality (notifications, password reset, account activation) is simulated for demonstration purposes only.
    \item \textbf{End-to-End Testing}: No automated browser-based testing of complete user workflows
    \item \textbf{Load Testing}: No systematic testing of system behavior under high concurrent load
    \item \textbf{Security Testing}: No automated penetration testing or security vulnerability scanning
    \item \textbf{Cross-Browser Testing}: Frontend components not tested across different browsers
    \item \textbf{Mobile Testing}: No testing of responsive design on actual mobile devices
    \item \textbf{Database Performance Testing}: No systematic testing of database performance under various load conditions
\end{itemize}

These limitations are acknowledged and represent areas for future enhancement as the system evolves toward production readiness.

\section{Development Environment}
\label{sec:development-environment}

This section provides guidance on setting up and using the development environment for the Solar Energy Monitoring and Control System.

\subsection{Setup Requirements}
\label{subsec:setup-requirements}

To set up the development environment, the following software is required:

\begin{description}
    \item[Java Development Kit (JDK)] Version 21 or later
    \item[Node.js] Version 20 or later
    \item[Docker and Docker Compose] For containerized development
    \item[PostgreSQL] Version 15 or later (or use Docker container)
    \item[Maven] Version 3.8 or later for building Java projects
    \item[Git] For version control
    \item[IDE] IntelliJ IDEA, Eclipse, or VS Code with appropriate plugins
    \item[Python] Version 3.9 or later for device simulation
\end{description}

\subsection{Quick Start Guide}
\label{subsec:quick-start}

\subsubsection{Prerequisites}
\label{subsubsec:prerequisites}

Before setting up the development environment, ensure you have the following installed:

\begin{description}
    \item[Docker \& Docker Compose] Latest stable version
    \item[Git] For version control
    \item[Text Editor/IDE] VS Code, IntelliJ IDEA, or similar
\end{description}

\textbf{Optional (for manual development):}
\begin{description}
    \item[Java Development Kit (JDK)] Version 21 or later
    \item[Node.js] Version 20 or later
    \item[Maven] Version 3.8 or later
    \item[PostgreSQL] Version 15 or later
    \item[Python] Version 3.9 or later (for device simulation)
\end{description}

\subsubsection{Fast Setup with Docker (Recommended)}
\label{subsubsec:docker-setup}

This is the fastest way to get the system running:

\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Clone the Repository:}
    \begin{verbatim}
    git clone https://github.com/2001J/nebular-power.git
    cd nebular-power
    \end{verbatim}
    
    \item \textbf{Configure Environment:}
    Create a \texttt{.env} file in the project root:
    \begin{verbatim}
    # JWT Authentication
    JWT_SECRET=your_jwt_secret_key_32_chars_minimum
    JWT_EXPIRATION=86400000
    
    # Admin User Setup
    ADMIN_EMAIL=admin@example.com
    ADMIN_PASSWORD=initial_admin_password
    \end{verbatim}
    
    \textbf{Generate JWT Secret:}
    \begin{verbatim}
    # Use OpenSSL to generate a secure key
    openssl rand -base64 32
    \end{verbatim}
    
    \item \textbf{Start All Services:}
    \begin{verbatim}
    docker-compose up --build
    \end{verbatim}
    
    \item \textbf{Access the Application:}
    \begin{itemize}
        \item Frontend: \url{http://localhost:3000}
        \item Backend API: \url{http://localhost:8080}
    \end{itemize}
    
    \item \textbf{Login:} Use the admin credentials from your \texttt{.env} file
\end{enumerate}

\subsubsection{Device Simulation Setup}
\label{subsubsec:simulation-setup}

To test the system with simulated solar panel data:

\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Configure Simulation:}
    Edit \texttt{pi\_simulation/config.json}:
    \begin{verbatim}
    {
        "general": {
            "simulation_name": "Solar Demo",
            "installation_id": "1",
            "server_url": "http://localhost:8080"
        }
    }
    \end{verbatim}
    
    \item \textbf{Set Up Python Environment:}
    \begin{verbatim}
    cd pi_simulation
    python -m venv venv
    
    # Activate virtual environment
    source venv/bin/activate    # macOS/Linux
    # venv\Scripts\activate     # Windows
    
    pip install -r requirements.txt
    cd ..
    \end{verbatim}
    
    \item \textbf{Run Simulation:}
    \begin{verbatim}
    chmod +x run_pi_simulation.sh    # macOS/Linux only
    ./run_pi_simulation.sh           # macOS/Linux
    # run_pi_simulation.bat          # Windows
    \end{verbatim}
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Development Setup}
\label{subsec:development-setup}

For active development work, you can run services individually:

\subsubsection{Manual Development Environment}
\label{subsubsec:manual-setup}

\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Start Database:}
    \begin{verbatim}
    docker-compose up -d postgres
    \end{verbatim}
    
    \item \textbf{Backend Development:}
    \begin{verbatim}
    ./mvnw clean install
    ./mvnw spring-boot:run
    \end{verbatim}
    
    \item \textbf{Frontend Development:}
    \begin{verbatim}
    cd solar_frontend
    npm install
    npm run dev
    \end{verbatim}
    
    \item \textbf{Access Points:}
    \begin{itemize}
        \item Frontend (dev): \url{http://localhost:3000}
        \item Backend API: \url{http://localhost:8080}
        \item Database: \texttt{localhost:5432}
    \end{itemize}
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Development Workflow}
\label{subsec:development-workflow}

\subsubsection{Branching Strategy}
\label{subsubsec:branching}

\begin{description}
    \item[main] Production-ready code
    \item[develop] Integration branch for features
    \item[feature/*] Individual feature development
    \item[hotfix/*] Critical production fixes
\end{description}

\subsubsection{Development Process}
\label{subsubsec:dev-process}

\begin{enumerate}
    \item Create feature branch from \texttt{develop}
    \item Implement feature with tests
    \item Submit pull request with:
    \begin{itemize}
        \item Clear description
        \item Test coverage
        \item Updated documentation
    \end{itemize}
    \item Code review and approval
    \item Merge to \texttt{develop}
    \item Deploy to staging for integration testing
\end{enumerate}

\subsubsection{Production Deployment}
\label{subsubsec:production-deployment}

\textbf{Docker Compose (Recommended):}
\begin{verbatim}
docker-compose -f compose.yaml up -d
\end{verbatim}

\textbf{Alternative Deployment Options:}
\begin{description}
    \item[Kubernetes] Container orchestration for large-scale deployments
    \item[Cloud Platforms] AWS, Azure, GCP with managed services
    \item[Hybrid] Combination of on-premises and cloud components
\end{description}

\textbf{Deployment Architecture Principles:}
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Containerization}: All components packaged as Docker containers
    \item \textbf{Statelessness}: Horizontal scaling capability
    \item \textbf{Configuration Management}: Environment-based configuration
    \item \textbf{Health Monitoring}: Built-in health checks
    \item \textbf{Security}: Secure communication between services
\end{itemize}



\section{Contributing Guidelines}
\label{sec:contributing}

\subsection{Code Standards}
\label{subsec:code-standards}

\subsubsection{Language-Specific Standards}
\label{subsubsec:language-standards}

\begin{description}
    \item[Java] Google Java Style Guide with project adaptations
    \item[TypeScript/JavaScript] ESLint with Airbnb style guide
    \item[Python] PEP 8 guidelines for simulation scripts
    \item[SQL] Consistent formatting and naming conventions
\end{description}

\subsubsection{General Principles}
\label{subsubsec:general-principles}

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Consistency}: Follow established patterns
    \item \textbf{Readability}: Self-documenting code with meaningful names
    \item \textbf{Maintainability}: Single responsibility principle
    \item \textbf{Testability}: Design code for easy testing
    \item \textbf{Documentation}: Document public APIs and complex logic
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Git Workflow}
\label{subsec:git-workflow}

\subsubsection{Branch Strategy}
\label{subsubsec:branch-strategy}

\begin{verbatim}
main
+-- develop
|   +-- feature/energy-monitoring-enhancement
|   +-- feature/payment-integration
|   +-- feature/security-improvements
+-- hotfix/critical-security-patch
\end{verbatim}

\subsubsection{Contribution Process}
\label{subsubsec:contribution-process}

\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Create Feature Branch:}
    \begin{verbatim}
    git checkout develop
    git checkout -b feature/your-feature-name
    \end{verbatim}
    
    \item \textbf{Develop and Test:}
    \begin{itemize}
        \item Write code following style guidelines
        \item Add comprehensive tests
        \item Update documentation
        \item Test locally
    \end{itemize}
    
    \item \textbf{Commit Changes:}
    \begin{verbatim}
    git add .
    git commit -m "feat(energy): add anomaly detection algorithm"
    \end{verbatim}
    
    \item \textbf{Submit Pull Request:}
    \begin{itemize}
        \item Clear description of changes
        \item Reference related issues
        \item Include test results
        \item Request appropriate reviewers
    \end{itemize}
\end{enumerate}

\subsubsection{Commit Message Format}
\label{subsubsec:commit-format}

\begin{verbatim}
<type>(<scope>): <subject>

<body>

<footer>
\end{verbatim}

\textbf{Types:} \texttt{feat}, \texttt{fix}, \texttt{docs}, \texttt{style}, \texttt{refactor}, \texttt{test}, \texttt{chore}

\textbf{Examples:}
\begin{verbatim}
feat(energy): implement real-time monitoring dashboard
fix(payment): resolve transaction validation issue
docs(api): update authentication endpoints documentation
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{Code Review Guidelines}
\label{subsec:code-review}

\subsubsection{Review Checklist}
\label{subsubsec:review-checklist}

\textbf{Functionality:}
\begin{itemize}
    \item Code works as intended
    \item Handles edge cases appropriately
    \item Error handling is comprehensive
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Quality:}
\begin{itemize}
    \item Follows coding standards
    \item Code is readable and maintainable
    \item Appropriate level of abstraction
    \item No code duplication
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Testing:}
\begin{itemize}
    \item Adequate test coverage
    \item Tests are meaningful and comprehensive
    \item All tests pass
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Documentation:}
\begin{itemize}
    \item Public APIs are documented
    \item Complex logic is explained
    \item README/documentation updated if needed
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Security:}
\begin{itemize}
    \item No security vulnerabilities
    \item Input validation is proper
    \item Authentication/authorization is correct
\end{itemize}

Code review comments should be constructive, specific, and actionable, with the goal of improving the code quality.

\section{Future Development}
\label{sec:future-development}

This section outlines planned enhancements and future directions for the Solar Energy Monitoring and Control System.

\subsection{Roadmap}
\label{subsec:roadmap}

The development roadmap includes:

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Advanced Analytics (Planned)}: Machine learning algorithms for energy usage prediction and optimization
    \item \textbf{Mobile Application (Planned)}: Future development of native mobile applications for iOS and Android
    \item \textbf{Integration Capabilities (Planned)}: API enhancements for integration with third-party systems
    \item \textbf{Enhanced Security (Planned)}: Advanced tampering detection and prevention mechanisms
    \item \textbf{Expanded Payment Options (Planned)}: Additional payment methods and financing models
    \item \textbf{Multi-language Support (Planned)}: Internationalization for global deployment
    \item \textbf{Weather Integration (Planned)}: Advanced weather data integration for improved energy forecasting
    \item \textbf{IoT Expansion (Planned)}: Support for additional IoT devices and protocols
\end{itemize}



\subsection{Architecture Evolution}
\label{subsec:architecture-evolution}

The architecture is expected to evolve in these directions:

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Event-Driven Architecture}: Transitioning from the current direct HTTP communication model to an asynchronous event-driven architecture using message brokers like Kafka or RabbitMQ. This will improve system resilience, enable better scaling, and allow for more loosely coupled modules. Implementation will include:
    \begin{itemize}
        \item Message queue integration for asynchronous processing of data
        \item Event sourcing patterns for capturing state changes as a sequence of events
        \item Real-time event stream processing using libraries like RxJava or Project Reactor
        \item Publish-subscribe patterns to decouple producers from consumers
    \end{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Serverless Components}: Introduction of serverless functions for specific workloads
    \item \textbf{Edge Computing}: Processing capabilities closer to the devices for reduced latency
    \item \textbf{Multi-Cloud Strategy}: Support for deployment across multiple cloud providers
    \item \textbf{Modular Refinement}: Further optimization of modules for better maintainability and scaling
    \item \textbf{GraphQL API}: Implementation of GraphQL for more flexible data retrieval
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Emerging Technologies}
\label{subsec:emerging-tech}

The system plans to incorporate these emerging technologies:

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Artificial Intelligence}: For anomaly detection, predictive maintenance, and energy optimization
    \item \textbf{Blockchain}: For secure, transparent transaction records and device identity
    \item \textbf{Advanced Encryption}: For enhanced data security and privacy
    \item \textbf{Digital Twins}: For simulation and optimization of physical installations
    \item \textbf{Augmented Reality}: For installation maintenance and troubleshooting
    \item \textbf{5G Connectivity}: For higher bandwidth and lower latency device communication
\end{itemize}

\section{Troubleshooting and Support}
\label{sec:troubleshooting}

This section provides guidance for developers on troubleshooting common issues and accessing support resources.

\subsection{Common Issues}
\label{subsec:common-issues}

Common development and operational issues include:

\begin{description}
    \item[Database Connection Issues:] 
        \begin{itemize}
            \item Check database credentials and connection string
            \item Verify database server is running and accessible
            \item Check network configuration and firewall settings
        \end{itemize}
    
    \item[Authentication Problems:] 
        \begin{itemize}
            \item Verify JWT secret configuration
            \item Check token expiration settings
            \item Validate user credentials in the database
        \end{itemize}
    
    \item[API Request Failures:] 
        \begin{itemize}
            \item Check request format and parameters
            \item Verify API endpoint URL
            \item Check authentication headers
            \item Examine server logs for detailed error information
        \end{itemize}
    
    \item[Device Communication Issues:] 
        \begin{itemize}
            \item Verify device token and installation ID
            \item Check network connectivity
            \item Validate request payload format
        \end{itemize}
    
    \item[Performance Problems:] 
        \begin{itemize}
            \item Check database query performance
            \item Monitor resource utilization
            \item Review caching configuration
            \item Optimize expensive operations
        \end{itemize}
\end{description}

\subsection{Logging and Monitoring}
\label{subsec:logging-monitoring}

The application uses Spring Boot's built-in logging capabilities for the backend and standard console logging for the frontend. These logs are essential for diagnosing issues during development and operation.

\subsubsection{Accessing Logs}
When running the application with Docker, you can view logs using:

\begin{verbatim}
# View backend logs
docker logs solar_backend

# View frontend logs
docker logs solar_frontend

# View database logs
docker logs postgres
\end{verbatim}

\subsubsection{Log Levels}
The application uses standard log levels (ERROR, WARN, INFO, DEBUG) which can be configured in the application properties:

\begin{verbatim}
# In application.properties
logging.level.root=INFO
logging.level.com.solar=DEBUG
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{Debugging Techniques}
\label{subsec:debugging}

Effective debugging techniques include:

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Log Analysis}: Reviewing application logs for error messages and stack traces
    \item \textbf{Remote Debugging}: Attaching debuggers to running services
    \item \textbf{API Testing}: Using tools like Postman to test API endpoints in isolation
    \item \textbf{Database Queries}: Examining database queries and execution plans
    \item \textbf{Network Analysis}: Monitoring network traffic with tools like Wireshark
    \item \textbf{Profiling}: Using profilers to identify performance bottlenecks
\end{itemize}

Example of enabling remote debugging for backend services:

\begin{verbatim}
java -agentlib:jdwp=transport=dt_socket,server=y,suspend=n,address=5005 \
     -jar target/solar-0.0.1-SNAPSHOT.jar
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{Support Resources}
\label{subsec:support-resources}

The primary support resource for developers is the project's GitHub repository at \url{https://github.com/2001J/nebular-power}, which contains:

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Detailed README files}: Each module has a dedicated README file with specific implementation details
    \begin{itemize}
        \item ENERGY\_MONITORING\_README.md
        \item PAYMENT\_COMPLIANCE\_README.md
        \item SECURITY\_TAMPERING\_README.md
        \item SYSTEM\_MONITORING\_README.md
        \item USER\_MANAGEMENT\_README.md
    \end{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Technical Documentation}: Located in the /docs directory, organized by user and developer documentation
    \item \textbf{Source Code}: Well-commented code with clear structure following the organization described in this document
\end{itemize}

For specific issues or questions, developers should refer to the appropriate module README or examine the corresponding test files, which provide practical examples of component usage.

\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:dev-conclusion}

The Solar Energy Monitoring and Control System represents a modern, scalable solution for monitoring and managing solar energy installations, with a particular focus on payment compliance and system security. The technical architecture and implementation details provided in this documentation should enable developers to understand, maintain, and extend the system effectively.

The modular monolithic architecture ensures that the system is maintainable and can evolve to meet changing requirements, while the comprehensive testing strategy helps maintain reliability and security. The documented APIs provide clear interfaces for integration with other systems, and the development guidelines ensure consistent, high-quality contributions.

As the system continues to evolve, the roadmap and architecture evolution plans provide a clear direction for future development, incorporating emerging technologies and addressing changing market needs. With the support resources and troubleshooting guidance provided, developers should be well-equipped to tackle any challenges that arise during development and operation.

By following the practices and principles outlined in this documentation, developers can contribute to the ongoing success and evolution of the Solar Energy Monitoring and Control System, helping to make solar energy more accessible and manageable for providers and customers alike.
